-- title:
--   The Lie algebra $L$ generated by the exchange Hamiltonians
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be the smallest real Lie algebra of $2^n\times2^n$ complex matrices, with bracket $[A,B]=AB-BA$, that contains $iE_{ij}$ for every pair of distinct qubits $i\neq j$:
--   $$
--   L=\operatorname{Lie}_{\mathbb R}\{\,iE_{ij}: i\neq j\,\}.
--   $$
--   A Hamiltonian $H$ can be obtained from the exchange interactions by real scalar multiplication, addition, Lie commutators and unitary conjugation exactly when $iH\in L$.
--
--   This is the algebra "generated by the exchange Hamiltonians" of Theorems 9.3.1 and C.2.1.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The book works with Hermitian Hamiltonians and the Hermitian bracket $i[A,B]$; the map $H\mapsto iH$ identifies that algebra with the real Lie span of the $iE_{ij}$, since $i\cdot i[A,B]=[iA,iB]$, and $\mathfrak{su}(d)$ becomes the traceless anti-Hermitian matrices. Unitary conjugation adds nothing: for $iH\in L$ and $X\in L$, $e^{-iHt}Xe^{iHt}=\exp(t\,\mathrm{ad}_{-iH})X$ stays in the finite-dimensional, hence closed, subspace $L$. Only real scalars are used, and $iI$ is **not** a generator.
-- source:
--   Bacon, Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1, Theorem 9.3.1, p.128 (PDF p.145); Theorem C.2.1, p.247 (PDF p.264)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_Collective_exchange

open scoped Matrix

attribute [local instance 100] LieRing.ofAssociativeRing

namespace BaconThesis.ExchangeUniv

/-- **The Lie algebra `L` generated by the BaconThesis.Collective.exchange Hamiltonians.**
Bacon, *Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers*, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1, Theorem 9.3.1, p.128 (PDF p.145): "let S be the set of BaconThesis.Collective.exchange Hamiltonians E_ij acting
between qubits i and j … If L is the Lie algebra generated by S"; Theorem C.2.1, p.247
(PDF p.264): Hamiltonians "obtained from BaconThesis.Collective.exchange interactions only via scalar multiplication,
addition, Lie-commutator and unitary conjugation".

`exchangeLie n` is the smallest real Lie subalgebra of `Matrix (Fin n → Fin 2) (Fin n → Fin 2) ℂ`
(bracket `[A, B] = AB − BA`) containing `i·E_ij` for every pair of distinct qubits `i ≠ j`.

**Formalization Note.** The book works with Hermitian Hamiltonians, real scalars and the
Hermitian bracket `i[A, B]`. The map `H ↦ iH` is a Lie algebra isomorphism from that algebra onto
the real Lie span of `{iE_ij}` used here (`i·(i[A, B]) = [iA, iB]`), so a Hamiltonian `H` is
obtainable exactly when `i • H ∈ exchangeLie n`, and `su(d)` corresponds to traceless
anti-Hermitian matrices. Unitary conjugation adds nothing: for `iH ∈ L`,
`e^{−iHt} X e^{iHt} = exp(t·ad(−iH)) X` lies in the finite-dimensional (hence closed) real
subspace `L` whenever `X ∈ L`. Only real scalars are allowed, and `iI` is not a generator. -/
noncomputable def exchangeLie (n : ℕ) :
    LieSubalgebra ℝ (Matrix (Fin n → Fin 2) (Fin n → Fin 2) ℂ) :=
  LieSubalgebra.lieSpan ℝ _ {A | ∃ i j : Fin n, i ≠ j ∧ A = Complex.I • BaconThesis.Collective.exchange i j}

end BaconThesis.ExchangeUniv


